<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    DEBUG BUILD TYPE ONLY. Chapter 30.

    Android has banned cleartext HTTP by default since API 28, which is why the
    first emulator run of this app rendered its shell perfectly and filled every
    data region with "Failed to fetch": the backend under test was
    http://10.0.2.2:8080 and the platform dropped the request before it left the
    device.

    This file lifts that ban for exactly one host and no others.

    WHY IT CANNOT AFFECT A RELEASE

    It lives in `src/debug/`, so Gradle merges it into the debug manifest and
    into no other variant. `assembleRelease` and `bundleRelease` never see it —
    not as an override that happens to be off, but as a file that is not part of
    the build at all. Verify with:

        ./gradlew :app:processReleaseManifest
        grep -c networkSecurityConfig app/build/intermediates/merged_manifests/release/AndroidManifest.xml

    WHY 10.0.2.2 AND NOTHING ELSE

    10.0.2.2 is the Android emulator's fixed alias for the host machine's
    loopback — it is meaningless on a real handset, so even this debug APK
    cannot be pointed at an attacker's server by DNS. `cleartextTrafficPermitted`
    is left at its secure default for every other destination, so a debug build
    that talks to the real API still requires HTTPS.

    `includeSubdomains` is absent deliberately: an IP literal has no subdomains,
    and setting it would only widen the rule for no benefit.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
